From 3ab25bc06768c92ed261c5c0f61648b3d2804a28 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 15:33:35 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 40/40] skippy: import stage checkpoints whole, and check
 recurrent imports

skippy_export_recurrent_state carries a session's recurrent memory alone:
since resident prefixes keep the attention cells as shared pool cells, the
attention half of a hybrid checkpoint travels as the KV page instead. Nothing
held importers to that. A hybrid session restored from the recurrent state
alone had no attention cells; it failed much later, at a page export, with a
message about missing token positions.

llama.cpp's own partial states have the same gap: LLAMA_STATE_SEQ_FLAGS_PARTIAL_ONLY
saves the recurrent and sliding-window halves, and the server keeps the
full-attention cells and the checkpoint's position range consistent by its own
bookkeeping. Here the native side checks against the live session instead.

- skippy_import_recurrent_state refuses a state that does not end where the
  session's attention cells do, when the stage holds attention layers, and
  leaves the sequence empty. A hybrid model's stage with no attention layers
  keeps empty attention cells and is not checked.
- skippy_model_checkpoint_parts reports what a checkpoint of the stage
  carries: a KV page, recurrent state, or both. It follows the stage's layers,
  so such a stage reports recurrent state alone.
- skippy_import_checkpoint takes the parts together, refuses any part the
  stage does not need and any it lacks, imports them in order with the
  position and token history, and empties the sequence again on failure.

The sampling-history snapshot restores through skippy_import_checkpoint. The
GraniteHybrid state test also checks that the recurrent state alone, and the
page alone, are refused and leave the session usable for the whole pair.
